{"ex-208-1":{"number":"1","title":"Tracing the position of a projectile","question":"<p>Tennis ball <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> falls freely from rest at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0. The crosses (×) in the</p><p>figure show the positions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0, 0.1 s, 0.2 s and 0.3 s.</p><p>Another tennis ball <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is projected horizontally from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0. The circle</p><p>(○) in the figure shows the positions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 and 0.2 s. In the figure,</p><p>mark the positions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.1 s and 0.3 s. Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/61ec0539a0c6e48e9605d50c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:302.0px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/64a1fd12f85076a8265a167b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:302.2px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Q moves uniformly in the horizontal direction. As it moves 4 grids in the horizontal direction in 0.2 s, it moves 2 grids in every 0.1 s. On the other hand, the vertical motion of Q is the same as P. Starting at the same height, the height of Q at any instant is always the same as P.</p>","think":"<p>Do balls P and Q have the same speed at t = 0.2 s?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>No. Their downward velocity components are equal: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. P has no horizontal component, whereas Q retains its non-zero horizontal velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mtext>&gt;</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 7","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":2},"id":"ex-208-1","chapter":"208"},"ex-208-2":{"number":"2","title":"Horizontal and vertical distances travelled","question":"<p>Gareth is 2.4 m away from a dartboard. He throws a dart horizontally with a speed of 8 m s<sup>−1</sup>. Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a247d521b9605379d182bbd4.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:355.6px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the time needed for the dart to hit the dartboard.</p><p>(b) The dart is launched when it is at the same level as the bullseye. Determine where on the dartboard the dart would hit.</p><p>(c) ‘If Gareth throws the dart horizontally, he will have to throw it at a higher level in order to hit the bullseye.’ Explain the statement.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p>Time needed = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 0.3 s</p><p>(b) Take the downward direction as positive.</p><p>Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>= 0 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 9.81 × 0.3<sup>2</sup></p><p>= 0.441 m</p><p>∴ The dart hits a point 0.441 m below the bullseye.</p><p>(c) The dart falls under gravity, so it always reaches the dartboard at a position lower than its initial position. Therefore, the dart should be thrown at a position higher than the bullseye.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Apply s<sub>x</sub> = u<sub>x</sub>t.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>From (a), the dart takes 0.3 s from launching to hitting the dartboard. In this journey, u<sub>y</sub> = 0 and a<sub>y</sub> = 9.81 m s<sup>–2</sup>.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 12","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":19},"id":"ex-208-2","chapter":"208"},"ex-208-3":{"number":"3","title":"Final speed of a projectile","question":"<p>A fire breaks out in a remote area. A helicopter carrying a bucket of water flies horizontally at a constant velocity of 40 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the right. The bucket is 30 m above the ground. Water is released when the bucket reaches position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The water then falls onto the fire. Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/07b5f90887f8f5c75659c91c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:266.9px\"/></p><p>(a) Where is the helicopter when the water reaches the fire?</p><p>(b) Find the horizontal distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and the fire.</p><p>(c) Find the speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the water when it reaches the fire</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Directly above the fire</p><p>(b) Take the downward direction as positive.</p><p>Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>30 = 0 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 9.81 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2.473 s</p><p>Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 40 × 2.473 = 98.9 m</p><p>(c) Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> + 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0 + 2 × 9.81 × 30 = 589</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> = 46.8 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/60187dac85eee740532a2e29.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:159.6px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The helicopter and the water have the same horizontal speed.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>It takes time t for the water to travel a vertical distance of 30 m; it also takes time t for the water to travel a horizontal distance s<sub>x</sub>.</p>","think":"<p>Should the water be released earlier or later if</p><p>(a) the helicopter flies at a smaller horizontal velocity;</p><p>(b) the bucket is hung at a higher position?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>(a) Later along the approach, when the helicopter is closer to the fire. At the same height the falling time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> is unchanged, but the required horizontal release distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is smaller. The time between release and arrival over the fire is still the same.</p><p>(b) Earlier, farther from the fire. A greater height increases the falling time, so at the same horizontal speed the water must be released at a larger horizontal distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":44},"id":"ex-208-3","chapter":"208"},"ex-208-4":{"number":"4","title":"Time of flight and range","question":"<p>In a football game, a player kicks the football at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> so that it flies off at an angle of projection of 30° with a speed of 45 m s<sup>−1</sup>. Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/bfd4cb11ca20e2268ac5b8cb.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:309.6px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the horizontal component <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and vertical component <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of the football’s initial velocity.</p><p>(b) Find the maximum height that the football reaches.</p><p>(c) If the football lands at the same level as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, what is the time of flight and the range?</p>","answer":"<p>Take the upward direction as positive.</p><p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> cos θ = 45 cos 30° = 39.0 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ = 45 sin 30° = 22.5 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/8c4192ee9f140a0bb8b7d056.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:156.4px\"/></p><p>(b) Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> + 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>0 = 22.5<sup>2</sup> + 2 × (−9.81) × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 25.8 m</p><p>The maximum height is 25.8 m.</p><p>(c) Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>0 = 22.5<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × (–9.81) × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4.59 s or 0 (rejected)</p><p>The time of flight is 4.59 s.</p><p>Consider the horizontal direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 39.0 × 4.59 = 179 m</p><p>The range is 179 m.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>v<sub>y</sub> = 0 at maximum height.</p>","think":"<p>If the football flies off with the same initial speed but a slightly larger angle of projection, how will the time of flight and the maximum height change?</p><p>Tip: Should we consider the horizontal direction and/or the vertical direction when finding each of the two quantities?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>Both increase. Increasing the angle slightly above 30° increases <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. For landing at the launch level, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so both the time of flight and the maximum height increase.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 17","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":77},"id":"ex-208-4","chapter":"208"},"ex-208-5":{"number":"5","title":"Angle of projection below the horizontal","question":"<p>Amy gives a smash in a volleyball match. She hits the ball at a position 2.7 m above the ground and 1.5 m away from the net. The ball leaves her hand at an angle of 15° below the horizontal with a speed of 35 m s<sup>−1</sup>. If the net is 2.2 m high, will the ball cross the net? Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7d7a42ca4cb9570cf4abddc7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:271.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the downward direction as positive.</p><p>When the ball has travelled from Amy by a horizontal distance of 1.5 m,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.0444 s</p><p>Consider the vertical direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>= (35 sin 15°) × 0.0444 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 9.81 × 0.0444<sup>2</sup></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/aded5495c04e11750a7a386e.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:239.9px\"/></p><p>= 0.412 m</p><p>Height of the ball above the ground = 2.7 − 0.412 = 2.288 m &gt; 2.2 m</p><p>∴ The ball will cross the net.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 18","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":110},"id":"ex-208-5","chapter":"208"},"ex-208-6":{"number":"6","title":"Properties of symmetrical trajectory","question":"<p>Teddy lobs a ball over the goalkeeper. The ball’s time of flight from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 1.4 s and its angle of projection is 60°. Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5b5a4c66838ab938a181251f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:309.6px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the initial speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the ball.</p><p>(b) The goalkeeper is at a position directly below the maximum point reached by the ball. Find the horizontal distance between the goalkeeper and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) State the velocity of the ball when it reaches the ground.</p><p>(d) If Teddy lobs the ball at an angle of projection of 30°, the ball will also head towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. State one reason for Teddy not to choose this angle.</p>","answer":"<p>Take the upward direction as positive.</p><p>(a) Consider the vertical motion of the ball from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p>0 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> × 1.4 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × (–9.81) × 1.4<sup>2</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 6.867 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 7.93 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(b) Time needed to reach the maximum height = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.7 s</p><p>Consider the horizontal motion of the ball from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to the maximum height.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> cos θ × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 7.93 cos 60° × 0.7 = 2.78 m</p><p>The horizontal distance between the goalkeeper and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 2.78 m.</p><p>(c) 7.93 m s<sup>−1</sup> (towards right, 60° below horizontal)</p><p>(d) If the ball flies at an angle of projection of 30°, it will reach a smaller maximum height. It will be easier for the goalkeeper to catch the ball.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":125},"id":"ex-208-6","chapter":"208"},"ex-208-7":{"number":"7","title":"Analysing projectile motion using law of conservation of energy","question":"<p>An object is at a height 3 m above the ground. It is projected at 12 m s<sup>−1</sup> at an angle of 10° above the horizontal and hits the ground. Neglect air resistance.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/4d175a0e2533c3ce863bbbea.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:198.8px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the maximum height above the ground that the object reaches.</p><p>(b) Find the speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the object when it hits the ground.</p>","answer":"<p>Take the PE at the ground as zero.</p><p>(a) Consider the motion from the starting position to the maximum height.</p><p>KE<sub>1</sub> + PE<sub>1</sub> = KE<sub>2</sub> + PE<sub>2</sub></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 12<sup>2</sup> + 9.81 × 3 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × (12 cos 10o)<sup>2</sup> + 9.81 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 3.22 m</p><p>The maximum height above the ground is 3.22 m.</p><p>(b) Consider the motion from the starting position to the ground.</p><p>KE<sub>1</sub> + PE<sub>1</sub> = KE<sub>2</sub> + PE<sub>2</sub></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> + 0</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 12<sup>2</sup> + 9.81 × 3 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 14.2 m s<sup>−1</sup></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>At maximum height,</p><p>v = u<sub>x</sub> = u cos θ</p>","think":"<p>Solve the above question using Newton’s laws of motion. Can you obtain the same results?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>Yes. With gravity as the only force, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The initial components are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>(a) At the highest point, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Δ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, and the height above the ground is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>sin</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) At ground level, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>x</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Both results agree with the energy method.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 208  |  Source PDF p. 23","source":{"name":"208 Examples.docx","sha256":"29e280f5f954dda814dd63637e189e610180e4bac6206064087ad82840a54a83","block":147},"id":"ex-208-7","chapter":"208"}}